*A new rhythm every 4 weeks*

## What you’ll learn in our African drumming classes

Every 4 weeks, we learn a new West African rhythm. First, you’ll learn 2 to 3 different parts of the rhythm on the [djembe](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Djembe). Then you’ll play those parts together as a group, alongside a solo djembe.

Along the way, you’ll also pick up:

- Call-and-response warm-ups and breaks.
- Djembe technique and the three essential sounds of the drum.
- How the ensemble parts fit together.
- The story behind each rhythm, and often the dance that goes with it.
{% /column %}

{% column %}
![Drummer playing dunun drums with sticks as students play djembes in an African drumming class](/media/2024/02/20210209_181706-scaled.jpg)
{% /column %}
{% /columns %}

{% section tone="dark" %}
## How each drumming class works

1. **Warm up:** First, we warm up with call-and-response patterns, so everyone settles into the groove.
2. **Learn the parts:** Next, you learn the parts of this month’s rhythm, one at a time.
3. **Play together:** Then we put the parts together as one ensemble, alongside a solo djembe.
4. **Breaks and solos:** Finally, we add the breaks and play the whole rhythm from start to finish.
{% /section %}

{% section tone="grey" %}
*Get fit · Have fun · Learn something new*

## Why join African drumming classes?

Drumming is good for your body, your mind and your social life. Here’s how a weekly drum class helps.

- **Fitness through rhythm:** Drumming gets your body moving, so you build strength and stamina while you have fun.
- **Coordination & memory:** Learning rhythms also challenges your brain, so your hands and memory get sharper every week.
- **Stress relief:** Playing drums helps you unwind and live in the moment. As a result, you’ll leave feeling lighter.
- **Community & friendship:** Above all, the rhythm of the drums brings people together, so class feels like a weekly catch-up with friends.
- **Culture & connection:** You’ll also learn the stories behind each rhythm, so you connect with the culture of West Africa.
{% /section %}

